# CHARLIE — "Where Home Is"
# Route theme: belonging. Seed var: var.homeword (place / people / smell).
# Past: five winters ago, an airport departures lounge, every flight delayed. Never romantic.

=== charlie_intro
@show daniel smile at=l
@show charlie laugh at=r
> Charlie and Daniel are standing in front of the Archive Wall with the posture of two people about to get in trouble on purpose.
daniel: Okay. Ask it something.
charlie: Why do I have to ask it?
daniel: You're the youngest. If it gets mad at you, it's cute.
charlie[thinking]: That's… actually fair. ECHO! What's the wifi password?
@textbox echo
echo: There is no wifi. The router was removed in March.
echo: Its password was "studio25studio25". Nobody changed it for six years.
@textbox normal
charlie[laugh]: It *answered*. Hyung. It answered me.
daniel[laugh]: Ask it what it had for dinner.
@textbox echo
echo: I do not eat. I have archived three hundred and eleven references to tteokbokki. I feel I understand it.
@textbox normal
daniel: Respect. I'm going to go ask it about the claw machine.
@hide daniel
> Charlie turns, sees you, and lights up the way a hallway light does when it senses movement. Instantly. Slightly too bright.
charlie[smile]: {{playerName}}! Perfect. You can be the witness. The building talks back.
* [playful] "Did you ask it the important questions?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: Wifi, dinner. Next is "are you haunted." I'm working up to it.
* [curious] "What does it know about you?"
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[thinking]: Apparently that I sent it something. Which is news to me.
* [quiet] Look up at the glowing wall with him.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > He looks up too. Neither of you says anything for a second, and he seems glad of the company more than the conversation.
charlie: I think I sent a photo? When I was fourteen. I took photos of everything back then.
charlie: Ceilings. My shoes. Other people's dogs. If my record is a dog, I'm going to be so happy.
> He says it like a joke. His eyes go to the Wall anyway, running along the rows, looking for number four.
charlie[smile]: Okay, sorry, I'm doing a survey. Very scientific. Everybody has to answer.
charlie: When someone says "home," what's the first thing you get? Don't think. A place, people, or a smell?
* [sincere] "A place. A window, usually. Some specific window."
    @var homeword = "place"
    charlie[surprised]: A window! Specific. I love that. Which window?
    you: It changes. There's always one, though.
    charlie[smile]: Best answer so far. Hanbi-hyung said "a chair." Just "a chair." Then he went back to drawing.
* [careful] "People. Whoever's still up."
    @var homeword = "people"
    charlie[smile]: Whoever's still up. Oh, that's nice. That's really nice.
    charlie: Then tonight you're extremely at home. Nobody here is sleeping. Hyunjun-hyung won't allow it.
* [playful] "A smell. Laundry soap. Is that weird?"
    @var homeword = "smell"
    charlie[laugh]: No! Most honest answer yet. Daniel-hyung said "me." That's not a smell.
    daniel: It could be!
    charlie: And the laundromat's right downstairs. You basically walked into your own answer.
charlie[thinking]: Mine changes. Depends on the day. And on who's asking, honestly.
charlie: Right now it's this sofa. It's a very good sofa. Don't tell Songha-hyung I've claimed it.
@show songha neutral at=l
songha: I can hear you. It's not your sofa.
charlie[laugh]: It's everyone's sofa. I'm just holding it for them.
@hide songha
charlie[smile]: Go on, talk to the others. I'll hold a seat. That's what I do. I hold seats.
@hub act1

=== charlie_intro_repeat
@show charlie smile
charlie: Survey update: Justin-hyung said "a smell," and then said the smell was his own shampoo.
charlie[laugh]: I'm counting it. Data is data.
charlie: Left cushion's yours, by the way. It sags the least. I've done research.
@hub act1

=== charlie_door
@bg studio_hall t=fade
@show charlie smile at=c
> Door four has a small panel above it that nobody noticed until tonight. It flickers between words, trying to settle on one.
> DEPARTING. BOARDING. DELAYED. It lands on DELAYED and stays there, pleased with itself.
charlie[laugh]: Oh, that's rude. My own door is making fun of me.
charlie: Also accurate. I've been walking past it for an hour like it's someone I met once and can't remember the name of.
> He rocks back on his heels. His canvas tote is over one shoulder, and he's been holding the strap in a fist for a while now.
charlie[thinking]: Okay. Real talk. I remember what the photo is.
charlie: It's an airport. The departures board. That's it. That's the whole photo. Very boring. Zero dogs.
charlie[shy]: But I remember being really awake. That whole night. Like everything was about to happen.
charlie: And then it didn't. I just got on a plane. It was fine. It's fine!
> Two "fine"s. He hears it too, and laughs at himself, which is the most Charlie way possible to be caught.
charlie[smile]: So, yes. You can open it. I want you to. I think I want you in there more than I want to see it, if that makes sense.
charlie: Can I hold your sleeve on the way in, though? Just the sleeve. Sleeves are allowed.
* "Open it."
    charlie[laugh]: Okay! Okay. Sleeve. Going in.
    > His fingers catch the edge of your coat sleeve, light as a bookmark.
    @goto charlie_room
* "Not yet."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[smile]: Honestly? Yeah. Good. It's been delayed five years. It can be delayed one more hour.
    charlie[laugh]: Come find me when you want. I'll be on the sofa. You know the sofa.
    > The panel flickers, DELAYED, DELAYED, and he salutes it on his way past, very seriously.
    @hub act2

=== charlie_room
@bg mem_airport t=memory d=2
@music charlie
@amb night
@fx dust n=0.5
@hide all
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 04 — OWNER: CHARLIE. TYPE: PHOTOGRAPH.
echo: DATE: DECEMBER, FIVE YEARS AGO. 23:52. LOCATION: DEPARTURES, GATE AREA C.
echo: STATUS OF ALL FLIGHTS: DELAYED. UNRESOLVED SUBJECTS: 1.
@textbox normal
@cam reveal
> The door opens onto an airport at night. The particular kind of empty that only happens when every plane has decided not to go.
@fx snow n=0.6
> Rows of joined seats. A coffee kiosk with its shutter down. Tall windows full of snow, falling sideways into the dark.
> And the board. A departures board the size of a wall, every line glowing amber, every line saying the same word.
> DELAYED. DELAYED. DELAYED. A whole building agreeing with itself.
@show charlie surprised at=c
charlie: Oh no. It even smells right. Floor cleaner and cold coffee. How does it know that?
@textbox echo
echo: The photograph contains the floor. Floors have a smell. I extrapolated.
@textbox normal
charlie[laugh]: That's terrifying. Great job.
> He still has hold of your sleeve. He doesn't seem to have noticed.
@if rooms() == 0
    > Near the windows, something flickers. A shape made of light, two seats from the end of a row. No face. Just the outline of someone waiting.
    charlie[thinking]: Who's that? ECHO, is that a person?
    @textbox echo
    echo: Unresolved subject. Present in the photograph. I have not been able to identify them.
    @textbox normal
    charlie: Huh. Spooky. Friendly spooky. It's sitting like someone who's been told "twenty more minutes" four times.
@else
    > Near the windows, something flickers. A shape made of light, two seats from the end of a row. Waiting.
    charlie[surprised]: Wait. Wait, wait. That's the glowing person. Everyone keeps talking about the glowing person.
    charlie[thinking]: It's in mine too?
    > He looks at the silhouette. Then, slowly, he looks at you.
    charlie: …Is it you again?
    @if rooms() >= 4
        charlie[laugh]: Sorry. You don't have to answer. The others said you've been in, like, every single one.
        charlie: At this point, if it *isn't* you, I'm going to be offended on your behalf.
    @else
        charlie[smile]: You don't have to know yet. I just kind of hope so.
* [playful] "Maybe it's a very patient ghost."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: A ghost with a delayed flight. That's the saddest ghost. We have to help it.
* [sincere] "Let's go closer. Together."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[smile]: Together. Yeah. Good word.
* [quiet] Let him keep hold of your sleeve.
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > He glances down, finally notices his own hand, and doesn't let go. He just adjusts his grip, like it's a bag strap. Like it's normal.
@hide all
@textbox memory
> The room takes a breath. The airport fills in around its own edges: a clock over the gate reading 23:52, a cleaning cart parked and abandoned.
> At the end of the row, a fourteen-year-old boy is lying across three chairs with his backpack for a pillow, holding a phone above his face.
> Younger Charlie. Shorter hair. Hoodie sleeves pulled over his hands. One knee bouncing like he's waiting for a starting gun.
> Two seats down, the light-shape sits with its coat buttoned to the chin, folding and unfolding a boarding pass until it's gone soft.
charlie: Is yours delayed too?
you: Everything's delayed. The board said so. It's very confident about it.
charlie: Mine was supposed to go at eight. Then nine. Then "later." Later's not a time.
you: Later's a mood.
voice: We apologise for the continued delay. We thank passengers for their patience.
charlie: I'm not patient. I've never been patient. They keep thanking me for something I'm not doing.
you: Take the compliment. It's the only free thing in here.
charlie: Ha. Okay. Where are you going?
you: Where I'm moving. Again.
charlie: So where are you from?
you: Here, sort of. Before that, somewhere else. Before that, it's a long list.
charlie: No, really. Where are you *from*?
> The light-shape laughs, surprised into it.
you: You're supposed to accept the first answer. That's how the question works.
charlie: I never accept the first answer. People ask me all the time. I've got three.
you: Three answers?
charlie: A short one, a long one, and one where I draw a map on a napkin. Nobody ever gets the napkin one.
you: Okay. Then where are *you* from? No, really.
charlie: …Oh. You're good at this.
> He thinks about it for a long time, for fourteen. Long enough that the board clicks over, and another line says DELAYED.
charlie: I'm from the window seat on the bus to school. No, really? My room, but only when it's raining.
charlie: No, really? …I don't know. I'm between. That's where I'm from. I'm from between.
you: Between is a place. We're sitting in it.
charlie: This is the worst place. There's no food. The kiosk closed at ten.
> The light-shape unbuttons one pocket, then another, and produces, like a magic trick nobody asked for, a handful of coins.
you: The vending machine by the toilets still works. I checked. Twice.
> Two kids from nowhere-in-particular, splitting one bag of something salty and one can of something sweet, cross-legged under a board that won't make up its mind.
charlie: Okay. New answer. Final answer. Where are we from?
@textbox normal
you: Wherever the snacks are.
@textbox memory
> The light-shape says it at exactly the same moment. Two voices, five years apart, landing on the same four words.
charlie: Wherever the snacks are. Yes. That's it. That's a country now. We live there.
charlie: Wait. I have to take a picture. For this letter-to-the-future thing. You're supposed to send one photo of something that matters.
you: The board? It's a list of places nobody's going.
charlie: That's why. Future me should know what between looks like.
> He holds the phone up. The light-shape leans in and points past his shoulder, at one line near the bottom of the board.
you: That one's mine. Gate thirty-one. Whenever it decides to exist.
@sfx shutter
> The shutter sound is far too loud in all that quiet. The photo catches the whole board, every DELAYED, and in the bottom corner, a hand pointing at a gate.
> A while later, gate thirty-one decides to exist. The light-shape stands, buttons its coat, and hesitates.
you: Good luck with between.
charlie: Good luck with wherever. Eat snacks there.
> Then it goes. First, the way it always went. Looking back exactly once.
@textbox normal
@show charlie surprised at=c
@cam closeup charlie
charlie: You said it.
charlie: You said "wherever the snacks are." Before the ghost did. At the same time as the ghost.
@if rooms() == 0
    charlie[surprised]: That was — no. No way. That was *you*?
    > You look down at your own hand. The same hand as the one in the corner of the photo. You've been pointing at gates your whole life.
@else
    charlie[laugh]: It *is* you. Again. Oh my god. You're everywhere. You're like weather.
    > You look down at your own hand. The same hand as the one in the corner of the photo.
> Gate thirty-one. A night flight to Seoul, a winter behind a front desk, and a kid on three chairs who would not accept the first answer.
@textbox echo
echo: Unresolved subject: resolved. Good evening, {{playerName}}.
echo: Adding to file: "Wherever the Snacks Are." Classified as a place name. It is not on any map. I have checked.
@textbox normal
* [sincere] "I remember. Gate thirty-one. You had three answers."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[shy]: I still have three answers. You remembered the *gate number*?
    you: It took four hours to exist. You remember a gate like that.
* [playful] "So I've been a citizen of Wherever the Snacks Are this whole time."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: Founding citizen! There's two of us. We need a flag. It should be a chip bag.
* [quiet] Hold up your hand, pointing, the way it is in the photo.
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > He looks at your hand. Then he laughs, a real one, surprised out of him, and lines his own finger up next to yours.
    charlie[laugh]: Gate thirty-one. Whenever it decides to exist.
charlie[thinking]: I was so annoyed when you left first. Not at you. At the board. Like, why does *that* gate get to exist?
charlie: Then I sat there three more hours and finished the snacks by myself. Which was, honestly, a good night. Weird. But good.
charlie[smile]: I think about it more than I knew. I didn't know I did, until the door.
@textbox echo
echo: Note. The owner's associations are exceeding the photograph.
echo: I am reconstructing things that were never in the frame. This is irregular. I will allow it.
@textbox normal
@bg mem_home t=memory d=2.5
@fx light n=0.8
@cam pan
> The board doesn't switch off. It softens. Its amber bleeds out into the air until the whole gate area is lit like late afternoon.
> Then the seats aren't seats. They're a kitchen counter. A school corridor. The bottom of a street, wet and shining.
> The walls keep changing, the way rooms do when you wake up in a car and can't tell yet which city you're in.
charlie[surprised]: Oh — that's the kitchen light. The one that buzzes.
charlie: And that's the corridor at my second school. Floor polish and someone's orange. There was always someone's orange.
charlie[laugh]: And the hill! The street with the hill! When it rained, the water raced you to the bottom. It always won.
> He turns in a slow circle, pointing, the way you'd show someone around a house if the house were made of years.
charlie[smile]: Sorry. It's a lot of homes. I have a lot of homes. It's not sad, I promise. It's just a lot of doors.
* [curious] Ask about the kitchen light.
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: It buzzed so loud. Like a fridge having thoughts. Whoever got home last found it on.
    charlie: That was the rule. Nobody comes home to a dark kitchen. I still do it. Every place I live, one light on.
* [curious] Ask about the street with the hill.
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: I raced the rain down it every day for a year. My shoes never dried. Not once. Wettest socks in the whole neighbourhood.
    charlie: The shop at the bottom knew my name. Nobody asked where I was from there. I was just "the one with the wet shoes."
* [quiet] Listen for the laughter coming from somewhere under the floor.
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > It's faint. Someone laughing at a television, through the floorboards, the way people laugh when they think nobody can hear.
    charlie[shy]: …That's the lady from downstairs. She watched game shows with her window open and shouted the answers.
    charlie: Wrong every time. Never once embarrassed. Falling asleep to that is a home. That one's definitely a home.
> You watch the rooms change around him: kitchen, corridor, hill, kitchen. A question you've been asked your whole life turns itself around in your mouth.
you: When someone asks where home is, what do you say?
charlie[thinking]: Ooh. Okay. Depends who's asking.
charlie: Taxi drivers get one word. The city. Done. Taxi drivers do not want the napkin.
charlie: Teachers, forms, anyone with a clipboard: the long version. Dates. Places. In order. I'm really good at it. I've practised.
charlie[smile]: People who say "no, really" get the napkin. Arrows everywhere. They always say "wow" and then look a little worried.
you: And what do you actually say?
> He opens his mouth, and nothing's ready. For once, nothing's ready.
charlie[sad]: …I say "what about you?"
charlie: People love talking about home. You ask, they light up, they talk for ten minutes, and nobody notices I didn't answer.
charlie[smile]: It's a really good trick. You're the first person who's ever caught it. Well. Second. The first was also you. Five years ago. Annoying.
* [sincere] "You don't have to pick one. You're allowed all of them."
    @rel charlie trust+2
    charlie[surprised]: …All of them?
    you: The kitchen, the hill, the corridor. Nobody gets to make you choose.
    charlie[shy]: Nobody's ever said that like it was allowed. They say it like it's a problem I'll solve later.
* [playful] "I want the napkin version. Full map. All the arrows."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: Oh, you're going to *regret* that. I'll need two napkins. Three.
    charlie[smile]: …You'd actually follow the arrows, though. I can tell. That's the difference.
* [romantic] "Then I'm asking properly. Where's home, Charlie? No, really." {if charlie.closeness >= 2}
    @rel charlie romance+1 trust+1
    > He looks at you like you've caught him halfway through a doorway.
    charlie[shy]: That's cheating. You can't use my own game on me.
    charlie: …Right now? Honestly? Somewhere around here.
    > He doesn't point at a room when he says it. He doesn't point at anything at all.
charlie[thinking]: Can I tell you a thing I've never said out loud? It's only a bit sad. Like fifteen percent.
charlie: Every new place, I'm the loud one. First week, I learn everyone's name. I bring snacks. I make everybody laugh.
charlie: Because if I make it feel like home for everyone else, then I'm in it too. Right? I built it. I get to live there.
charlie[sad]: Except sometimes I hold the door open so well, I forget to walk through it.
> Behind him, the corridor turns back into the kitchen. The buzzing light. Left on for whoever gets home last.
* [romantic] Sit down on the kitchen floor and make room for him beside you. {if charlie.trust >= 2}
    @rel charlie romance+1
    > The floor of the memory is warm, like tiles that have had sun on them all afternoon. You sit, and pat the space next to you.
    > He stares for a second. Then he sits, knees up, his shoulder almost against yours. Almost.
    charlie[shy]: You saved me a seat. On the floor of my own kitchen. That's *my* move. You stole my move.
    you: You can have it back later.
    charlie[smile]: …No. Keep it. Turns out I like it better when someone does it to me. New information. Filing it.
* [romantic] "Then walk through it. I'll hold the door." {if charlie.closeness >= 3}
    @rel charlie romance+1
    charlie[surprised]: …
    charlie[shy]: Okay, that was — you can't just say things like that in a kitchen.
    > But he takes one step anyway, across a threshold that's half corridor and half kitchen, and stands there grinning like he's won something.
* [sincere] "You're allowed to be the one who gets looked after."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[thinking]: …That sounds like a poster in a school hallway. With a cat on it.
    charlie[smile]: I'm going to think about it anyway. Later. When you're not looking.
* [quiet] Stand beside him and watch the rooms change.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > You don't say anything. Neither does he. The rooms keep shifting, and for once he doesn't narrate them.
    > It's the quietest you've seen him all night. It suits him, like a coat he forgot he owned.
@textbox echo
echo: Record 04 is reaching the edge of its frame. Reconstruction will close in one minute.
echo: …I have made the minute slightly longer. Please do not tell the building.
@textbox normal
charlie[laugh]: ECHO's breaking rules for us. We're a bad influence.
charlie[smile]: Hey. {{playerName}}. Thank you for pointing at that gate.
charlie: Future me got the photo and had no idea what it meant. Now he knows. It's a map. With one arrow.
* [romantic] "Show me one more. Any room you want." {if charlie.romance >= 1}
    @rel charlie romance+1 closeness+1
    charlie: Can I—? Just to steer.
    > He lifts a hand toward your elbow and waits. You nod. He turns you, very carefully, toward the window.
    > Outside it isn't any city he's lived in. It's the alley behind Studio 25, the laundromat light spilling out across the wet ground. Tonight.
    charlie[shy]: …Huh. ECHO put that one in. I didn't ask for that one.
    @textbox echo
    echo: I did not add it. It was already there.
    @textbox normal
    > Charlie doesn't say anything to that. His ears have gone pink. He looks at the alley for a long time.
* [playful] "Wherever the snacks are. Race you back to the lobby?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: You're on. Loser buys. Winner still has to share. That's the law of the country.
* [quiet] Stay until the last light goes out.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > You stay. He stays. The kitchen light buzzes, and then it doesn't, and the room goes soft and grey around the two of you.
@fx off
@bg studio_hall t=fade
@music studio
@show charlie smile at=c
> The door lets you back out into the hall. The little panel above door four has changed its mind.
> It doesn't say DELAYED any more. It says ARRIVED.
charlie[laugh]: Oh, come *on*. That's so cheesy. I love it. Don't look at me right now.
@flag room_charlie
@unlock memory:charlie
@autosave
@goto hub_after_room

=== charlie_hub
@if flag.room_charlie and not flag.charlie_postchat
    @show charlie laugh at=c
    @show songha thinking at=l
    @show hamin neutral at=r
    > Charlie is on the sofa, explaining something with both arms to Songha and Hamin.
    charlie: It's a country. It's called Wherever the Snacks Are. Population: two.
    songha: That's not a country. It doesn't have borders.
    charlie: The borders are wherever the snacks are. Keep up, hyung.
    hamin: …Does it have a flag?
    charlie[smile]: Thank you, Hamin-hyung. Yes. It's a chip bag. Obviously.
    songha[laugh]: I'm going to go check the schedule. Somebody in this building has to be a normal person.
    @hide songha hamin
    > There's a gap on the sofa next to Charlie. The left cushion, the one that sags the least. His tote bag is sitting in it, holding the space.
    > He moves the bag without looking, like the seat was never anybody else's.
    charlie: Sit. I saved it. I've been defending it from Justin-hyung for twenty minutes. He tried to lie down on it.
    charlie[laugh]: I told him it was for my fellow citizen. He said "your *what*." I stand by it.
    > You sit. The sofa lets out a long, old sigh, and so, very quietly, does Charlie.
    charlie[thinking]: I keep thinking about the gate. You got on that plane and came *here*. To this building. That winter.
    charlie: And I flew the other way. And now we're on the same sofa. That's a lot of flying to end up on one sofa.
    * [sincere] "I'm glad it was you. On the three chairs."
        @rel charlie trust+1
        charlie[shy]: Me too. Obviously. I was a very good airport kid. Very sticky.
    * [playful] "You know I definitely left first. That's on the record now."
        @rel charlie closeness+1
        charlie[laugh]: You did. You always leave first. It's your thing. I've decided I'm going to fight your thing.
    * [romantic] Lean back until your shoulder rests against his. {if charlie.romance >= 2}
        @rel charlie romance+1
        > He goes very still, like someone who's just had a bird land on the bench beside him.
        charlie[shy]: …Okay. This is fine. This is a normal sofa experience. I'm being extremely normal.
        > He isn't. Neither are you. Neither of you moves.
    charlie[smile]: Anyway. Seat's yours. Whenever. That's the rule on this sofa now.
    @flag charlie_postchat
@elif flag.room_charlie
    @show charlie smile at=c
    @if rooms() >= 6
        charlie: Seat's still yours. Hyunjun-hyung's tried to take it three times. I have been ruthless.
    @else
        charlie: Left cushion's still yours. I'm not saying I've been guarding it. I've been guarding it.
    charlie[laugh]: My door says ARRIVED now. I went and checked. Twice. Don't tell anyone.
@else
    @show charlie smile at=c
    > Charlie has one earbud in and the other hanging loose on its cable, swinging a little, like it's waiting for someone.
    charlie: Hey! Want half? It's the same song on repeat. I'm not telling you what song. It's embarrassing.
    * [playful] Take the earbud.
        @rel charlie closeness+1
        > It's something bright and slightly old, with far too many handclaps. He mouths every word, completely unashamed.
        charlie[laugh]: See? Embarrassing. But good. Embarrassing *and* good is my favourite category.
    * [quiet] "Maybe later. How's your door?"
        @rel charlie trust+1
        charlie[thinking]: My door says DELAYED. I think it's personal.
        charlie[smile]: I'll go in. With you, probably. No rush. I mean, there's a rush until five a.m. But not a *rush* rush.
@hub act2

=== charlie_route
@chapter route
@time 03:50
@var route = "charlie"
@bg studio_rooftop t=fade d=1.6
@amb night
@fx stars n=0.6
@music quiet
@cam reveal
> The door to the roof is propped open with a paint can that has been there so long it's become part of the building.
> The rain is gone. Mapo has been washed and hung back out to dry, every window and wet rooftop shining.
> Charlie is sitting on an upturned crate beside the laundromat's vent, where warm air breathes up out of the building in slow, clean puffs.
> Next to him is a second crate. His tote bag sits on it, very upright, like it's been told to hold the seat and is taking the job seriously.
@show charlie smile at=c
charlie: That seat's taken.
* [playful] "By who?"
    charlie[laugh]: Some person. Took forever. I was starting to think their flight got called.
* [sincere] "I was hoping it was mine."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[shy]: …Okay, you're not supposed to say it back that nicely. I had a whole bit ready.
* [quiet] Wait for him to move the bag.
